WEBVTT

NOTE
Narration of the Agent Kourier incident demo (the incident demo repository's film, narration.json).
Cue times from the build's placement of each line, split at the pauses in its audio.

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.666
This is a staged outage in a test cluster,

2
00:00:03.666 --> 00:00:07.310
handled in Slack by three AI agents and
one person.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.795
Checkout and payments are two teams, each
with its own channel and agent.

4
00:00:12.795 --> 00:00:17.385
They share an incidents channel, which has
its own agent: an incident commander.

5
00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:20.590
Agent Kourier connects each channel to its
agent,

6
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.720
which runs on kagent in Kubernetes.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.389
Alertmanager posts alerts in the checkout
channel.

8
00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:30.275
When an agent escalates, a pager bot posts
in the next channel,

9
00:00:30.275 --> 00:00:32.060
and the agent there picks it up.

10
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.420
The failure:

11
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.276
payments v2.14.0 changes the amount field
from a whole number of cents to a

12
00:00:40.276 --> 00:00:40.276
string.

13
00:00:40.276 --> 00:00:44.330
Checkout can't decode the response, so
every checkout fails.

14
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.317
Nobody types a prompt.

15
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:51.879
The agents investigate, escalate, page,
and propose a rollback.

16
00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:53.740
One person approves it.

17
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.033
The alert lands in the checkout channel.

18
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:00.920
Agent Kourier opens a thread and hands it
to checkout's agent.

19
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.133
It investigates in the thread. Each tool
call is a step:

20
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:08.420
resources, logs, metrics, traces.

21
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.044
Its finding:

22
00:01:10.044 --> 00:01:16.080
payments v2.14.0, rolled out three minutes
earlier, changed amount to a string.

23
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.691
How did it know it was payments?

24
00:01:18.691 --> 00:01:22.795
Checkout's own pods were healthy, and
unchanged for nine hours.

25
00:01:22.795 --> 00:01:27.225
Its calls to payments were failing to
decode, yet payments reported no errors.

26
00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:29.140
And payments had just rolled out.

27
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.049
To find who owns payments, it looked the
service up in the service catalog:

28
00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:37.440
the Payments team, their channel, and who
is on call.

29
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.684
The escalation tool doesn't take the
agent's word for it.

30
00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:44.669
It re-checks the metrics and the rollout
itself,

31
00:01:44.669 --> 00:01:48.490
and posts to the incidents channel only
when every check passes.

32
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.026
In the incidents channel, the incident
commander declares the incident,

33
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:54.980
and pages the owning team.

34
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.078
The page lands in payments ops, naming the
on-call engineers,

35
00:01:59.078 --> 00:02:01.530
and the payments agent takes it from
there.

36
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.440
It checks the rollout history and the
metrics, then reads the source.

37
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.526
It lists the commits in the v2.14.0
release,

38
00:02:10.526 --> 00:02:14.714
opens the diff of the one that touched the
response, and reads its pull request.

39
00:02:14.714 --> 00:02:16.125
The diff confirms it:

40
00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:18.640
amount went from an integer to a string.

41
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:25.000
It proposes a rollback to v2.13.0, and
asks for approval before running it.

42
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.133
Lee, the secondary on-call, approves in
the thread,

43
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.100
and the agent runs the rollback.

44
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.910
Errors fall below one percent, and the
alert resolves.

45
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.971
The incident commander writes the summary:

46
00:02:38.971 --> 00:02:42.090
the timeline, the cause, and the follow-
ups.

47
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.948
Alert to recovery took about five minutes.

48
00:02:47.948 --> 00:02:52.530
The one step a person took was the
approval. That's a per-tool setting:

49
00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.018
a team could let the agent roll back on
its own,

50
00:02:55.018 --> 00:02:57.320
and the Binding decides who may approve.
